# sf-audit Naming Convention Config
#
# Copy this file to sf-audit-naming-config.md in your working directory
# to override the default naming conventions for your org.
#
#   cp sf-audit-naming-config.md.example sf-audit-naming-config.md
#
# The skill will detect this file automatically and use these rules
# instead of the built-in defaults.
# This file is excluded from git (.gitignore) — it is org-specific.

---

## Apex Class Suffixes

Classes must end in one of these recognised suffixes. Classes without
a matching suffix will be flagged as violations.

- `_CTRL` — Visualforce / Aura controllers
- `_SERVICE` — Business logic service classes
- `_HANDLER` — Apex trigger handler classes
- `_BATCH` — Batch Apex jobs
- `_TEST` — Test classes
- `_SCHEDULER` — Scheduled Apex jobs
- `_SELECTOR` — Data access / query classes
- `_BUILDER` — Builder / factory classes
- `_FACTORY` — Factory pattern classes

## Generic Class Names to Flag (standalone words only)

Flag any class whose entire name is one of these generic words:

- Utility, Utils, Manager, Common, Misc, Helper

## Apex Trigger Naming

- Required pattern: `[ObjectName]Trigger`
  Example: `AccountTrigger`, `ContactTrigger`
- Multiple triggers per object: **not allowed** (flag as violation)

## Custom Field Violations

Flag a field if its API name (before `__c`) matches any of these:

- Single characters: `A`, `X`, `Z` etc.
- Numeric-only descriptors: `Field1`, `F1`, `Col2`
- Generic standalone words: `Test`, `Temp`, `Flag`, `Data`, `Misc`, `Value`

## Flow Naming

Recommended pattern: `[Object]_[Action]_[Trigger]`
Example: `Account_SendWelcomeEmail_AfterInsert`

Flag a flow if:
- API name is a single word with no underscores
- Label is generic: `Flow`, `New Flow`, `Untitled`, `My Flow`, `Test Flow`

## Validation Rule Naming

Flag a rule if:
- Name is fewer than 3 segments (e.g., `Rule1`, `VR_1`)
- Name is a generic word: `Check`, `Validate`, `Error`
